  <h1>History of Politics in Shetland</h1>

  <div class="essay">
    <p>As of 2016, the electorate of Shetland have voted on six different major elected bodies. In the present day, Shetlanders vote on the Parliament of the United Kingdom, the Scottish and European Parliaments and the Shetland Islands Council. Former representative bodies include the Parliament of Great Britain, the Parliament of Scotland, the <a href="/council/lerwick-town-council">Lerwick Town Council</a> and <a href="/council/zetland-county-council">Zetland County Council</a>.</p>

    <h2>The Tings</h2>

    <p>The earliest recorded history of political ongoings in Shetland dates to the Picts and the Viking eras, with pre-parliament and council deliberation taking part in the ancient Tings, the Viking name for an assembly site. Such was the importance of Tings, areas of Shetland were named to include 'ting' in their name. This is still true to the present day, with areas such as Tingwall, Lunnasting and Westing still carrying the suffix 'ting'. It was at these Norse parliaments that a group of select representatives would meet with the Earl, such as the Law Ting Holm in Tingwall, the site of Shetland's parliament until the 16th century.</p>

    <p>The Holm saw the first recorded mass gathering of islanders. In February 1577 the Privy Council sent commissioners to sit there and hear complaints against Laurence Bruce, and over 700 people came from almost every corner of Shetland to give evidence. Bruce had tried to get his defence in first: his court clerk had toured the parishes collecting marks on a testimonial in his favour, from people who could not read what they were putting their mark to.</p>

    <h2>Scotland and the Orkney connection</h2>

    <p>In the 13th Century, Shetland fell under the rule of the Parliament of Scotland. However, Shetlanders were entirely disenfranchised from the process of selecting their representatives in Scotland's Parliament. Instead, their neighbouring island, Orkney, held the reins. From 1235 until 1999, Shetland was inextricably connected to Orkney in national parliaments. Orcadians elected, appointed, and were appointed to represent both islands in first the Parliament of Scotland, and in the <a href="/council/parliament-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a> and the <a href="/council/parliament-uk">Parliament of the United Kingdom</a> (Westminster).</p>

    <p>Shetland in the 18th century was a society which was made up of a small number of elite, and large quantities of poor, uneducated citizens. The Lairds of the land had rule, but even the Shetland Lairds couldn't influence the appointment of the Member of Parliament; it was all choreographed from Orkney by around 40 men.</p>

    <p>Distance did the rest. When the old Scottish Parliament summoned someone to appear before it, the notice period was 48 hours in Edinburgh, 15 days for the rest of Scotland, 40 days for Orkney and Shetland, and 60 days for anyone abroad. In the eyes of the law, Shetland sat closer to foreign parts than to Fife.</p>

    <p>The electorate could be tiny. In November 1760 the roll for Orkney and Shetland carried a grand total of seven names, every one of them an Orcadian. Shetland wasn't even told when elections were happening: a parliamentary committee found in 1791 that the rule requiring notice of an election to be posted in Shetland had been ignored since the Union, nearly a century before. The complaint that brought this to light was dismissed as "frivolous". Shetland's lairds spent the next decades trying to win a vote of their own, and found the Orkney side hard to pin down. More than once, the Orcadians called a meeting to discuss it and failed to raise a quorum.</p>

    <h2>The Great Reform Act</h2>

    <p>In 1832, Westminster passed a law which reformed the British electoral system, known as the Great Reform Act. It came as a response to criticism of the existing system, which put the power of voting into the hands of the few. There was previously no secret ballot and votes were bought and sold. The reforms still meant that only men who owned property worth at least &pound;10 (close to &pound;1,000 in 2016 money), but these reforms meant that for the first time, some members of the Shetland community were finally able to have a say in electing their own representatives.</p>

    <p>This was to the annoyance of the Orkney elite whose power had been weakened significantly. To further compound the anger of Orkney's elite, the first general election that Shetlanders were able to participate in, the 1832 election, saw the Shetland-preferred candidate, George Traill, win the election against the Orkney preferred candidate.</p>

    <p>Getting there was an adventure in itself. One reform campaigner, writing as "Thulensis", had worked out that a freeholder from Unst travelling to Kirkwall to vote would face a round trip of 1,484 miles. When the vote finally came, Shetland polled at home, in Lerwick and Burravoe, on 7 and 8 January 1833, but the poll books still had to reach Kirkwall by sea in midwinter. The steamer hired for the job was a Clyde vessel that took such a battering on the way north that she put into Inverness for repairs. A replacement collected the books, and an estimated 3,000 Orcadians gathered in Kirkwall expecting a declaration. The Sheriff refused to make one until Shetland's votes arrived. They took their time. Even the History of Parliament, the official reference work, records this election as unopposed; it was anything but. It also had a third candidate, Robert Hunter of Lunna, the first Shetlander ever to stand for the seat. No report of the hustings mentions him, and he seems not to have been there at all.</p>

    <p>Fifteen years later the islands produced a candidate with a better story. Arthur Anderson had started out as a beach boy on Bressay, scrubbing fish and laying them out on the rocks to dry. He left Shetland at 16 and came back in 1847 as Managing Director of P&amp;O, to take on the sitting Member, Frederick Dundas. He did it in style, chartering a steam yacht to tour the isles; for some remote communities it was the first steamboat they had ever seen. In 1884 the vote was widened again, and Shetland's roll went from 467 names to 3,464, sevenfold, almost overnight.</p>

    <p>Starting from 1826, every election was contested on party grounds. Despite George Traill being replaced by Thomas Balfour, who was a Tory, Orkney and Shetland was a Liberal constituency for 98 years until 1935. The Conservative party (Unionist) represented the Isles from 1935 until it was reclaimed by the Liberals in 1950. The seat has remained Liberal (and Liberal Democrat since 1983) since then.</p>

    <h2>The Lerwick Town Council</h2>

    <p>On a local level, formal political representation did not begin until the 19th century, with the establishment of the <a href="/council/lerwick-town-council">Lerwick Town Council</a> in 1818. An agreement was reached in 1815 to turn Lerwick into a Burgh of Barony, and in 1818, the first meeting and election of the Lerwick Town Council occurred. The early Town Council elected 11 members and within that group, two were elected as Senior and Junior Baillies. The growing pains of the early councils meant that they were not particularly effective, and the electing of councillors was hardly a democratic process. The Lerwick Town Council minute book lists those eligible to vote for each election, with the numbers well under 60 men. Almost every single councillor was a member of Shetland's noble class, or was related to the elite. Elections were held every three years until 1876 when they moved to a yearly election cycle, with a total of 12 councillors serving 3-year terms, and four vacancies to fill every year.</p>

    <p>That first election, in April 1818, was by ballot of a sort: each elector wrote his choice on a slip of paper and signed it, so every candidate could find out exactly who had voted for him and, more to the point, who had not. The man elected Junior Bailie, Charles Ogilvy, turned the post down flat. Before the year was out, the Collector of Customs had called the Chief Magistrate, Arthur Edmondston, a "damned scoundrel", and Edmondston was seeking up to &pound;1,000 in damages.</p>

    <p>The council was also broke. With no money to pay anyone to clean the streets, it divided the town into districts and auctioned the manure, and whoever bought a district's manure had to keep that district clean. From 1824 councillors who missed a meeting without excuse were fined a shilling, and some meetings still failed to raise a quorum. Its agenda tells you what Lerwick was like. In 1850 it met to work out how to stop young men dragging burning tar barrels through the streets, a custom that would grow into Up Helly Aa. During the cholera scare of 1866 it wrote to the town's MP asking for a warship to keep the Dutch fishing fleet away from the inhabitants.</p>

    <p>Some names never seemed to leave. For 55 years the name John Robertson appeared in the council minutes, often twice: an uncle and his nephew, the younger of whom grew up in the South Trance, a house and shop that arched across what is now Commercial Street, so that the whole town walked through his home. Then, in September 1874, came the strangest election in the council's history. Lerwick held two at once, in the same building, under two different sets of rules. "To make matters doubly sure," the Shetland Times observed, "we had a double election." Two months later, two businesses in the town were tarred.</p>

    <p>Between the years of 1818 to 1833, the Town Council recognised things which they wanted to change &mdash; the poor sanitary conditions, the rowdy sailors &mdash; but it wasn't until 1833 when they founded the Commissioners of Police of the Burgh that they began to get a grip on the situation, albeit a very loose one. The problems continued throughout the 1800s, but the Town Council did begin to effect a change, with such things as improved schooling, sanitary conditions, regular water and policing all improving towards the latter part of the century.</p>

    <h2>The Zetland County Council</h2>

    <p>In 1889, Westminster passed the Local Government (Scotland) Act which provided that a county council should be established in each county. In 1890, the <a href="/council/zetland-county-council">Zetland County Council</a> held its first ever election, electing 27 councillors to represent the various wards Shetland had been subdivided into. For many in the peripheral communities of Shetland, this was the first real representation that they had experienced. The ZCC was less dominated by elites than the Town Council had been, but initially landowners and other wealthy Shetlanders still held most of the power. The ZCC wards included three seats for different areas of Lerwick, which means that Lerwick was doubly represented. This anomaly was eventually corrected in 1929, when the Lerwick seats were converted into a Parish District Council with much reduced powers.</p>

    <p>The first County Council election, in 1890, was fought on real grievances. In the autumn of 1888 several hundred whales had run ashore at Hoswick, and the laird claimed the traditional third share of the proceeds. The men who had taken the whales went to court, with a notice on the Sandwick chapel doors declaring "Landlord tyranny must be rooted out. Union is strength". When the County Council came along, one of their leaders, Sinclair T. Duncan, stood for Sandwick against the laird's brother. Elsewhere the new machinery creaked. In Aithsting and Sandsting the voters' rolls were labelled wrongly, so the north of the district voted for the south's candidate and vice versa. In Delting North a candidate wrote to the papers after nominations had closed to say he couldn't serve, and the election went ahead regardless. A Montrose town councillor stood for Bressay, arguing that living nearer Edinburgh was an advantage. Close contests went on for decades: in Sandness in 1929 the two candidates finished on 45 votes each, and the returning officer had to cast the deciding vote himself.</p>

    <h2>Ice cream, drink and brown water</h2>

    <p>Back in Lerwick, town elections around 1900 could barely get anyone out. In 1899 so few people turned up that at one o'clock the town crier was sent round with his bell to remind ratepayers that there was an election on; even then, only 105 of a possible 816 voted. Two years later a young auctioneer, James Robertson, stood as the town's first socialist candidate and treated Lerwick to what the paper called its first "platform oratory on municipal matters".</p>

    <p>What got people out was the issues, and they weren't always the ones you'd expect. In 1897 the council was handed a 12-foot petition signed by 257 people demanding that the town's shops stop selling ice cream on a Sunday. The argument ran for 17 years and went to a 5&ndash;5 tie broken by the Provost's casting vote, and two Town Council elections were decided entirely on it. After the First World War a record 13 candidates stood for seven seats, months after Provost Goodlad had said Lerwick was "too small a part of the country to have anything to do with unions". In 1920 an election circular accused the Labour councillors of trying to buy a &pound;1,200 road roller; when they protested, Provost Ganson dismissed their complaint as "purely flimsy flapdoodle". That December the town voted 945 to 432 to close its pubs, and Lerwick went dry in 1921, with consequences that reached Winston Churchill.</p>

    <p>In November 1931 Lerwick woke up to clear water running from its taps for the first time, and much of the town wanted its peaty brown water back. A Ratepayers Association was formed to bring it back, and a door-to-door canvass found 1,743 households for brown and 410 for white. At the 1933 election the Association put up the first two women ever to stand for Shetland's top tier of local government, Rose Sutherland and Charlotte Nicol, both teachers. When the Second World War stopped elections, the council filled each vacancy by taking the next-placed candidate from the 1938 poll. One man was co-opted five times over the years, once just three days after the voters had turned him out.</p>

    <h2>The Shetland Islands Council</h2>

    <p>Both the ZCC and the LTC continued to operate until August 1975 when both were replaced by the <a href="/council/shetland-islands-council">Shetland Islands Council</a>. This was a body created under the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973 and had 22 constituencies (6 of which were assigned to Lerwick, causing upsets to some).</p>

    <div class="credits">
      <h2>About this project</h2>
      <p>The Shetland Political History Project is an ongoing body of work aimed at building a comprehensive and easily accessible database of the history of politics in the Shetland Islands.</p>
      <p>The information was compiled by James Stewart with help from the Shetland Museum and Archives, Bayanne, Shetland Library and countless other contributors who have supplied information. All contributions from the public are welcomed.</p>
      <p>The primary interest of this work is the 18th, 19th and 20th century, so contemporary elections may be missing.</p>
